Title: THE VELVETEEN RABBIT
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from "The Velveteen Rabbit"
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - DAY

A cozy room filled with painted wooden blocks, a tall toy cupboard, and a brick fireplace fender.

VELVETEEN RABBIT—a plump stuffed toy with brown-and-white spotted velvet, thread whiskers, and pink sateen ears—sits propped beside the fender.

Beside him rests SKIN HORSE, an ancient leather toy horse, bald in patches with exposed seams and a thin hairless tail.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid. He was fat and bunchy, as a rabbit should be. But living in the nursery, he felt very insignificant and commonplace.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby.

Velveteen Rabbit looks down at his smooth velvet paws.

SKIN HORSE
But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

NANA, a firm nursemaid in a white apron, rummages through an open toy cupboard in a hurry.

THE BOY, seven years old, sits under the bedsheets looking sad.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

Nana grabs Velveteen Rabbit by one ear and plops him into the Boy's arms.

The Boy hugs Velveteen Rabbit tightly against his chin and pulls the blanket over them both.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Bright sunshine covers a manicured lawn framed by raspberry canes.

The Boy pushes Velveteen Rabbit in a small wooden wheelbarrow.

Later, Velveteen Rabbit rests inside a tiny hut of twigs built beneath the leafy raspberry bushes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Spring came, and wherever the Boy went, the Rabbit went too. Rides in the wheelbarrow, picnics on the grass, and fairy huts built behind the flower border.

Night falls over the lawn. Dew sparkles on the grass.

Nana walks across the lawn carrying a lit candle, scanning the damp grass until she spots Velveteen Rabbit.

Nana scoops up the muddy, wet toy and wipes his nose with her apron.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Nana carries the damp Velveteen Rabbit into the bedroom. The Boy sits up in bed, reaching out both hands.

NANA
Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

Velveteen Rabbit lies tucked under the Boy's arm. His glass eyes glisten under the mantelpiece night-light.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that, he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last. The nursery magic had happened to him.

EXT. WOOD - EVENING

Golden dusk filters through high oak trees and thick green bracken.

The Boy places Velveteen Rabbit comfortably in a patch of ferns, then runs off down the forest path to play.

Velveteen Rabbit sits motionless in the bracken.

Two wild hares—WILD RABBIT and a companion—creep out from the thick foliage. Their fur is soft, their noses twitch, and their hind legs shift effortlessly.

Wild Rabbit hops close to Velveteen Rabbit, sniffing the air.

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

Velveteen Rabbit sits frozen, his seamless pincushion tail buried in the leaves.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to.

Wild Rabbit stretches his neck and stares closely at the stuffed toy.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

WILD RABBIT
Stretch them out and show me!

Wild Rabbit whirls around in a dizzying dance on his hind feet, then stops short and brushes his whiskers against Velveteen Rabbit's nose.

Wild Rabbit flattens his ears and leaps backward.

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Heavy footsteps echo along the path. The Boy crashes through the brush.

With a flash of white tails, the wild rabbits leap away into the shadows.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Silence fills the woods. Only tiny ants crawl across Velveteen Rabbit's worn velvet paws.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - DAY

The curtains are drawn tight. The Boy lies in bed with flushed cheeks, breathing heavily.

Velveteen Rabbit remains tucked beside the Boy's feverish face under the thick blankets.

DOCTOR, wearing a dark suit, stands over the bed with Nana. He feels the Boy's forehead.

DOCTOR
The fever has broken at last. He may get up tomorrow.

NANA
And the room, sir?

DOCTOR
Everything must be disinfected. All the books and toys he played with in bed must be burnt.

Nana picks up the edge of the bedsheet, exposing Velveteen Rabbit.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. Get him a new one.

EXT. BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

A rusty iron bin and piles of garden weeds sit behind an old wooden coop.

A canvas sack rests open on the ground, stuffed with old picture books and Velveteen Rabbit.

Velveteen Rabbit wriggles his head out past the sack opening. His velvet coat is thin, threadbare, and gray.

He stares toward the distant raspberry thicket swaying under the moonlight.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A single clear tear wells in Velveteen Rabbit's button eye, rolls down his shabby velvet nose, and drops onto the soil.

Where the tear hits the ground, a green shoot sprouts instantly.

A golden cup-shaped flower unfolds from the emerald leaves.

A glow illuminates the blossom as NURSERY FAIRY—wearing a gown of glittering dew—steps out onto the grass.

Nursery Fairy kneels and gently scoops Velveteen Rabbit into her arms. She kisses his worn nose.

NURSERY FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I think so.

NURSERY FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out, I come and take them away and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

EXT. FOREST GLADE - NIGHT

Bright moonlight illuminates a velvet lawn surrounded by tall trees.

Several wild rabbits dance together in a ring.

Nursery Fairy floats down into the center of the glade, holding Velveteen Rabbit.

The wild rabbits stop dancing and gather around.

NURSERY FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

Nursery Fairy places Velveteen Rabbit gently onto the soft grass.

NURSERY FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

Velveteen Rabbit stays perfectly still, keeping his tail tucked under him.

An itch twitches on his nose. Without thinking, he lifts his hind foot to scratch it.

He freezes in wonder. Soft brown fur covers a real hind leg with flexible joints and sharp claws.

He twitches his ears—they move freely. Long white whiskers brush against the grass blades.

Velveteen Rabbit leaps high into the air, landing softly on his powerful hind legs. He spins and bounces across the turf with pure delight.

He turns back toward the center of the glade—Nursery Fairy has vanished into the moonlight.

EXT. WOOD - DAY

Warm spring sunlight filters through green leaves. Wild flowers carpet the forest floor.

The Boy, now healthy and taller, walks along the forest path holding a small wooden stick.

From behind the tall bracken, two wild rabbits peek out. One has soft brown fur; the other carries distinct white and brown markings across his back.

The Boy stops and gazes at the spotted rabbit.

THE BOY
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The spotted rabbit twitches his nose, twirls on his hind legs, and bounds happily deep into the woods.

The Boy smiles, watching him go.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the others, back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.
